.TH PIX 1 "July 2026" "pix" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pix \- run the pi coding agent in a Docker sandbox and manage its host services
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pix
.br
.B pix
.B run
.RI [ DIR ]
.RI [ flags ]
.RI [ \-\- " pi-args..." ]
.br
.B pix
.I DIR
.br
.B pix
.I command
.RI [ args ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pix
is the launcher for the
.B pi
coding agent in a Docker Sandbox managed by
.BR sbx (1).
It also controls the host-side memory service on port 11435 and knowledge
service on port 11436.
.PP
With no command,
.B pix
shows status.  It never launches a sandbox.  Launching is explicit through
.B run
or through the bare existing-directory form.  On a host without a configuration
file, the no-argument form points you at
.B pix run
(which launches a normal session without onboarding).
A bare non-directory word is an error, not a workspace name.
.SH PREREQUISITES
Two things are required before an agent can run:
.TP
.BR sbx (1)
The Docker Sandboxes CLI must be installed and on
.BR PATH .
It creates and runs the sandbox and injects provider keys through its network
proxy.
.TP
.BR op (1)
Guided setup requires the 1Password CLI, signed in.  It is the ONLY
provider-key source.  One configured provider is sufficient.  Setup validates
the configured Anthropic, OpenAI, or Google references, stores only those
references in pix, and reconciles the resolved values into host-managed
.B sbx
secrets.  Resolved keys are never written to pix configuration or shown
in its output.  There is no "trust existing sbx keys" shortcut (the removed
flags for it are hard errors).
.SH QUICK START
The shortest path from nothing to a fully configured agent:
.PP
.nf
.RS
brew install mcavage/tap/pix
pix setup
.RE
.fi
.PP
.B setup
configures at least one callable inference backend and launches Pix. API keys
use 1Password; a healthy existing Ollama or custom gateway can be keyless.
On a fresh machine (no sandbox exists yet) it also
launches the one-shot onboarding tour; if a sandbox already exists for the
directory it is left alone by default (never force-removed, never replayed
into a live session) unless
.B \-\-replace
is given, and if sbx cannot even be probed setup fails closed after the host
phase.  Later,
.B pix run
launches or reattaches a normal session without replaying onboarding.
.SH ONBOARDING
Onboarding is EXPLICIT, by name:
.B pix setup
is the real two-phase entry (see its own section below); it hands off to a
ONE-SHOT guided tour up front, not a progressive teach-as-you-go flow (say
"onboard me" to the agent inside a running sandbox to redo the tour any
time).  Setup's host phase seeds your identity (read from git's global
config) into the trusted host-state payload injected directly into the
launcher-generated kickoff prompt, deterministically, and best-effort into
the memory service.  Setup reports honestly which of the two actually
happened.  The in-session tour reads that trusted payload (never a workspace
file) and tells you the exact HOST command for anything not yet configured;
it does not write host config on your behalf.  (Separately, if a
.B .pix/onboarding.json
proposal file exists in the workspace, the host validates it against an
allowlist and applies it under a confirmation gate on the next run.)
.PP
.B pix run
on its own NEVER onboards and never forces it.  Its only concession is
bootstrapping a model key when none is set at all (resolving your 1Password
refs into sbx, steering you to 1Password on a TTY).  Provider keys,
.BR serve ,
and the memory service are host-side prerequisites.  For automation and CI,
.B "pix setup \-\-no\-agent"
applies deterministic host config from flags without a sandbox, a prompt, or
the agent handoff.
.SH CREDENTIAL MODEL
There are two distinct kinds of secret, and they live in different places.
.PP
Provider keys (Anthropic, OpenAI, Google, GitHub) are injected into every
sandbox by the network proxy from
.B sbx
secrets stored on the host.  By default the model keys are sourced from
1Password:
.B pix setup
collects an
.B op://
ref per provider, mirrors the refs into
.B op-refs.env
(sandbox) and
.B hostmode.env
(host mode), and reconciles the sbx secrets to match.  1Password is the
source of truth, sbx is where the proxy reads from.  Key values never appear
in a configuration file or inside the sandbox.  You can still set an sbx
secret by hand, but a later
.B pix setup
will reconcile it against your 1Password refs.  1Password is always the
source of truth (the removed skip-1Password shortcut flags are hard errors).
.PP
Integration credentials (for host MCP servers such as Slack) are 1Password
references kept in
.BR op-refs.env .
The file stores only
.B op://vault/item/field
references, never the secret value; the value is read from 1Password by
.BR op (1)
at the moment the integration starts and passed in as an environment variable,
so it never touches disk.  Some integrations, such as Google Workspace on
macOS, can use the system keychain instead and need no refs entry.  Manage the
refs template with
.BR "pix secret" .
.SH COMMANDS
.SS "status, st"
.TP
.BR "pix status" " [" \-\-json "]"
Show a read-only snapshot of services, provider keys, knowledge bundles, MCP
registration, the active pack, and running
.B pix-*
sandboxes.  The
.B \-\-json
option emits the snapshot as JSON.  This is also the no-command action.
.SS ls
.TP
.BR "pix ls" " [" \-\-json "]"
List the
.B pix-*
sandboxes on this host (name, state, workspace dir).  For every sbx sandbox, use
.BR "sbx ls" .
.SS rm
.TP
.BR "pix rm" " " name... " [" \-\-all "] [" \-\-except " " name "]"
Remove pix sandboxes (via
.BR "sbx rm \-f" ).
Scoped to
.B pix-*
names.
.B \-\-all
removes every pix sandbox;
.B \-\-except
keeps one (e.g. the box you are in).
.SS setup
.TP
.BR "pix setup" " [" DIR "] [" \-\-no\-agent "] [" \-\-apply "] [" \-\-replace "] [" \-\-pull\-models "] [" \-\-account " " email "] [" \-\-knowledge " " path-or-url "] [" \-\-mcp " " name "] [" \-\-model " " ollama-model "] [" \-\-yes "]"
Actually sets you up: a real, two-phase command.
Phase 1 (host) ALWAYS runs first, even on a repeat invocation. Setup asks how
models should run: API keys (the default), a healthy detected Ollama, or a
custom gateway. Direct provider keys come from 1Password; keyless gateway and
Ollama paths do not require
.BR op .
Memory is enabled only after Ollama's watcher and embedding models are verified.
An ordinary value flag
.RB ( \-\-account ,
.BR \-\-knowledge ,
.BR \-\-mcp ,
.BR \-\-model )
does not change the inference choice. In non-interactive mode the default is
the direct API-key path; therefore
.B \-\-yes
(alias
.BR \-\-non-interactive )
still requires valid 1Password references unless a pack preconfigures a
keyless backend.
.PP
Local Ollama models (watcher/embed/bridge) are probed once and reported on
their own readiness axis in the completion summary.  Setup only downloads a
CONFIRMED-missing tag with explicit consent:
.B \-\-pull\-models
in any mode, or a single default-No interactive prompt.  A non-interactive
setup (including
.BR \-\-yes )
never downloads without
.BR \-\-pull\-models ,
and an unverifiable
.B "ollama list"
is never treated as missing.  Setup never installs Ollama itself.
.PP
pix setup \-\-pull\-models with Ollama down exits 1.
pix setup with the same Ollama down exits 0 with an optional ⚠ row.
Stale optional config never blocks unrelated repair.
.PP
Ordinary setup prints a short action-oriented transcript.  \fB\-\-verbose\fR
exposes the underlying eight phases \(em parse, inventory, gate, mutate,
consent, verify, report, handoff \(em and the commands they run for diagnosis.
Setup sequences prompts one at a time and never prompts at all without a TTY.
Mutations run in a
fixed order with the riskiest last (keys, config, pack, MCP, knowledge,
identity, Google Workspace, model pulls); each is individually idempotent, so
an interrupted run is resumed by re-running the same command \(em setup
re-probes what is actually there rather than reading back a journal of what it
once intended.
.PP
.B \-\-no\-agent
runs the HOST phase only: no sandbox, no handoff.  It is the scripted/CI path
and replaces the removed
.B onboard
verb;
.B \-\-yes
and
.B \-\-non-interactive
stay orthogonal to it.
.B \-\-apply
applies a pending
.B .pix/onboarding.json
in DIR (the control-plane proposal an in-sandbox onboarding agent writes),
under a confirmation gate.
.PP
Setup validates one
.B op://
ref per provider, mirrors them into
.B hostmode.env
(host mode) and reconciles them into sbx (the sandbox), so both worlds share
the same source.  Host mode (pi UNSANDBOXED) is NOT provisioned or enabled by
setup; it is opt-in via
.B "pix host setup"
(which provisions AND enables it in one step \(em see
.BR "pix host" ).
Phase 2 (agent) depends on the sandbox state for DIR, and only a POSITIVE
answer acts: a confirmed-ABSENT sandbox gets a fresh launch handed off to a
ONE-SHOT guided tour (not progressive/teach-as-you-go); a confirmed-EXISTING
sandbox without
.B \-\-replace
is left alone (never force-removed, never replayed into a live session)
and setup instead prints
.B "pix run [DIR]"
(reattach as-is; the sandbox keeps its create-time pack/MCP/skills) or
.B "pix setup [DIR] \-\-replace"
(recreate with your current settings and receive the tour).  With
.B \-\-replace
setup relaunches through
.B "run \-\-replace"
carrying the tour kickoff, so the recreated sandbox actually gets it
(\-\-replace is harmless when the sandbox is absent).  If the sandbox state
cannot be determined at all (sbx errored or is missing), setup FAILS CLOSED
after the host phase (it never launches blind) and tells you to fix sbx
and re-run.
.SS doctor
.TP
.BR "pix doctor" " [" \-\-json "]"
Check inference backends and credentials, Ollama and its configured models, memory, Google Workspace,
1Password refs, and MCP registrations.  Print repair commands for failed checks.
.B \-\-json
emits a machine-readable report.
.SS run
.TP
.BR "pix run" " [" DIR "] [" \-\-dev "] [" \-\-skills " " DIR "]... [" \-\-kit " " K "]... [" \-\-mcp " " M "]... [" \-\-name " " N "] [" \-\-model " " M "] [" \-\-replace "] [" \-\- " " pi-args... "]"
Launch a sandbox for
.IR DIR ,
which defaults to the current directory.  An explicit directory must exist.
Arguments after
.B \-\-
are passed to
.BR pi .
.PP
Lifecycle matches sbx's own re-attach model.  When no sandbox of the resolved
name exists, it is created and the flags below apply.  When one already
exists, running or stopped,
.B run
RE-ATTACHES to it as-is instead of refusing or recreating: sbx reads the agent
from the existing sandbox's own spec, so
.BR \-\-kit ,
.BR \-\-mcp ,
.BR \-\-template ,
and the create-only skill flags are not re-sent.  Use
.B \-\-replace
to force a recreate instead.
.TP
.B \-\-dev
Use the local checkout kit and load its skills live.  A checkout must be found
through
.BR PIX_DEV_ROOT ,
the current directory or an ancestor, or the launcher's installed location.
Applies only to a fresh create; use
.B \-\-replace
to pick it up on an existing sandbox.
.TP
.BI \-\-skills " DIR"
Mount and load an additional skill tree.  Repeatable.  Create-only; see
.BR \-\-replace .
.TP
.BI \-\-kit " K"
Replace automatic kit selection with a path or git URL.  Repeatable.
Create-only; see
.BR \-\-replace .
.TP
.BI \-\-mcp " M"
Attach an MCP server when creating the sandbox (emitted to sbx as
.BR \-\-static\-mcp ;
served by the local data-plane gateway, no
.B SBX_MCP_URL
needed).  Repeatable; create-only (attach to a running sandbox with
.BR "pix mcp load" ).  See
.BR \-\-replace .
.TP
.BI \-\-name " N"
Use
.I N
as the sandbox name.  Otherwise the launcher derives
.BI pix- workspace .
.TP
.BI \-\-model " M"
Pass
.I M
to
.B pi
as its active model.  Unlike the other flags above, this is
.B not
create-only: it is a pi runtime argument, so it still reaches
.B pi
on a re-attach too.
.TP
.B \-\-replace
Recreate the sandbox (
.B sbx rm \-f
then create) instead of re-attaching to an existing one, whether it is running
or stopped.  This is the way to pick up a changed
.BR \-\-kit / \-\-mcp /
create-only flag.
.SS host
.TP
.BR "pix host" " [" DIR "] [" \-\-model " " M "] [" \-\- " " pi-args... "]"
EXPERT, DANGEROUS.  Run
.B pi
directly on this machine \(em no sandbox, no network fence, real credentials.
A narrow escape hatch for developing pix itself (which needs the host's
Docker, sbx, and make); never a fallback when
.B sbx
is missing.  Gated off by default: set it up deliberately with
.BR "pix host setup" ,
which provisions the host agent AND enables the gate in one step (the gate
stays off unless provisioning succeeds).  Turn it back off with
.BR "pix config set host.enabled false" .
The launch refuses a workspace at
.BR $HOME ,
.BR / ,
or
.BR /etc ,
or one inside or containing SSH/cloud/password-store secret directories
(symlinks are resolved before the check).  Subagent spawning is disabled, a
host-guard extension is always loaded, and a host-specific system preamble
replaces the sandbox's disposable-VM context.  These are guardrails against
accidents,
.B not
a security boundary \(em for anything you wouldn't hand a shell to, use
.BR "pix run" .
Cloud keys come from op:// refs in
.B hostmode.env
next to config.toml, resolved just-in-time by
.BR "op run" ;
without that file the session is Ollama-only (valid).
.TP
.B "pix host setup"
Provision the host agent directory at
.B $XDG_STATE_HOME/pix/host-agent
(default
.BR ~/.local/state/pix/host-agent ):
symlinks the checkout's harness dirs (skills, agents, extensions, themes), a
host-specific
.BR settings.json ,
a
.B sessions/
directory, and the curated pi extension packages (printing the exact install
commands when
.B pi
is unavailable).
.TP
Naming: this verb
.RB ( "pix host" ,
the agent running ON the host) is distinct from
.B pix-host
(the compiled Go daemon binary) and from
.B "pix serve"
(which runs the host services memory/knowledge).  Three names, three things.
.SS serve
.TP
.BR "pix serve" " [" args... "]"
Execute
.B pix-host serve
with unchanged arguments.  With no service arguments, the host configuration
selects services.  Memory is enabled by default; knowledge runs when enabled.
.TP
.B "pix serve stop"
Read the supervisor pidfile and verify that the process is
.BR "pix-host serve"
before sending SIGTERM.  If neither /proc nor ps can identify the process, it
REFUSES to signal (remove the pidfile to override) rather than trust the pidfile
alone.  It sends SIGKILL only if the process does not exit after about five
seconds and a repeated identity check still confirms the process is ours.
Extra arguments are rejected.
.TP
.BR "pix serve status" " [" \-\-json "]"
Report the supervisor PID and memory and knowledge port state.
.B \-\-json
emits JSON.
.TP
.B "pix serve start"
Alias for
.BR "pix serve install" ,
provided as the natural counterpart to
.BR "pix serve stop" .
.TP
.B "pix serve install"
Install the services as a MANAGED login service: a launchd LaunchAgent
.RB ( com.pix.serve )
on macOS, a systemd \-\-user unit
.RB ( pix-serve.service )
on Linux.  Starts at login, auto-restarts, and starts immediately.  Logs go to
.I ~/.local/state/pix/serve.log
on both macOS and Linux \(em the SAME file the lazy auto-start uses, so there
is one place to look regardless of how serve was started (on Linux,
.RI ( "journalctl \-\-user \-u pix-serve" )
also works as a secondary view).  Idempotent; re-running reinstalls with the current binary path.
Before installing, a lazily-started background daemon is stopped first and a
FOREGROUND
.B pix serve
is refused (stop it and re-run) \(em installing over a live daemon would make
the auto-restarting unit crash-loop on its ports.  After installing, the
command verifies (bounded) that the services actually came up and reports
honestly when they did not.  The generated unit captures the install-time
environment the daemon needs to read the SAME config and ports as the
launcher: an absolute
.B PIX_CONFIG
is always rendered, plus
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME ,
.BR MEMORY_DB ,
.BR MEMORY_PORT ,
.BR KNOWLEDGE_PORT ,
and
.B OLLAMA_HOST
when set at install time.
.TP
.B "pix serve uninstall"
Remove the managed login service (bootout/disable and delete the unit file).
.PP
LAZY AUTO-START: when the managed service is not installed,
.BR "pix run" ,
.BR "pix memory" ,
and
.B "pix knowledge query"
auto-start a detached
.B pix-host serve
if the configured service ports are down (the ollama-CLI model), waiting
briefly for health and printing progress.  Its log is
.IR ~/.local/state/pix/serve.log .
Opt out with the
.B PIX_NO_AUTOSERVE
environment variable or
.BR "pix config set host.autoserve false" .
After a daemon-affecting config write
.RB ( services ,
.BR memory_watcher_model ,
.BR memory_embed_model ,
.BR knowledge_bundles )
the launcher restarts a managed or lazily-started daemon automatically so the
change takes effect; a foreground serve is never killed \(em you are told to
restart it.
.B "pix knowledge init"
and
.B "pix knowledge use"
save
.B knowledge_bundles
too and propagate the same way.
.SS memory
The aliases
.BR mem ,
.BR search ,
.BR add ,
.BR rm ,
.BR promotable ,
and
.B status
map respectively to
.BR memory ,
.BR recall ,
.BR remember ,
.BR forget ,
.BR learnings ,
and
.BR stats .
.TP
.BR "pix memory recall" " " query " [" \-\-limit " " N "] [" \-\-project " " P "] [" \-\-json "]"
Search stored facts.  The default limit is 8.  The default project filter is
empty.  Short aliases
.B \-n
and
.B \-p
are accepted for
.B \-\-limit
and
.BR \-\-project .
.TP
.BR "pix memory remember" " " text... " [" \-\-json "]"
Store a fact with source
.B cli .
.TP
.BR "pix memory forget" " " id " [" \-\-json "]"
Delete the fact matching an ID or ID prefix.  The
.B \-\-json
option emits the raw result.
.TP
.BR "pix memory learnings" " [" \-\-min " " N "] [" \-\-json "]"
List recurring, promotable learnings.  The default minimum frequency is 3.
.TP
.BR "pix memory stats" " [" \-\-json "]"
Print counts by kind and durability.
.PP
Memory RPC commands return status 3 when the memory daemon cannot be reached.
Full backup and restore are now the top-level
.B backup
and
.B restore
verbs, not memory subcommands.
.SS backup
.TP
.BR "pix backup" " [" \-\-out " " PATH "] [" \-\-keep " " N "]"
Create a hot, verified FULL backup, safe while
.B serve
holds the database.  It packages a SQLite
.B "VACUUM INTO"
snapshot of the memory database, the
.B config.toml ,
the
.B op-refs.env
(references only), and a
.B manifest.json
(recording a note of each configured
knowledge bundle's path and git remote) into a gzip tar archive.  Knowledge
bundle contents are NOT archived: a bundle is a git repository and git is its
backup; the manifest records only where it lives.  The default output is
.BR ~/.local/share/pix/backups/pix-backup- timestamp .tar.gz;
the default retention count is 7.  The alias
.B \-o
is accepted for
.BR \-\-out .
The command execes the sibling
.B pix-host
binary and propagates its status.
.SS restore
.TP
.BR "pix restore" " " archive " [" \-\-force "]"
Restore a FULL backup by safe swap.  The command refuses to run while the memory
service is listening and refuses to replace an existing database without
.BR \-\-force .
With
.B \-\-force
(the alias
.B \-f
is accepted), it moves the current database to a timestamped backup first.  The
.B config.toml
and
.B op-refs.env
are always moved aside to a timestamped
.B .bak
file before the archived versions are written, so the change is reversible.  A
format-version-1 (memory-only manifest) archive still restores.  The command
reports the restored memory rows and the recorded knowledge-bundle locations,
then execes the sibling
.B pix-host
binary and propagates its status.
.SS knowledge
The alias
.B kb
maps to
.BR knowledge ;
.B search
maps to
.BR query ;
and
.B push
maps to
.BR sync .
.TP
.BR "pix knowledge init" " [" DIR "]"
Scaffold an OKF bundle, initialize Git when available, add it to
.BR knowledge_bundles ,
and enable the knowledge service.  The default directory is
.B knowledge
under the configuration directory.  Existing bundle files are not overwritten.
.TP
.BR "pix knowledge use" " " path-or-git-url
Set a global bundle reference.  A local path is made absolute but is not checked
for existence or OKF contents.  A Git URL is cloned or updated below the
configuration directory in
.BR knowledge-cache .
.TP
.BR "pix knowledge use" " " \-\-project " " path-or-git-url " [" \-\-dir " " D "]"
Write
.B .pix/knowledge
under
.I D
(default current directory) without changing global configuration.
.TP
.BR "pix knowledge ls" " [" \-\-json "]"
List the configured bundles, service state, and any project
pointer.  JSON output is selected with
.BR \-\-json .
.TP
.BR "pix knowledge query" " " text... " [" \-\-limit " " N "] [" \-\-json "]"
Search the daemon within the configured bundles.  The default limit is 5;
.B \-n
is an alias for
.BR \-\-limit .
An unreachable daemon returns status 3.
.TP
.BR "pix knowledge sync" " [" \-m " " MSG "] [" \-\-bundle " " D "] [" \-\-allow-main "]"
Commit and push a bundle.  Without
.BR \-\-bundle ,
exactly one bundle must be configured.  The default
commit message contains a UTC timestamp.  By default, create and push a
.B knowledge/sync-*
review branch and print a
.B gh pr create
hint.
.B \-\-allow-main
instead commits and pushes the current branch; detached HEAD is refused.
.B \-m
is an alias for
.BR \-\-message .
.TP
.BR "pix knowledge remote" " [" \-\-bundle " " D "]"
Show the selected bundle's origin URL.
.TP
.BR "pix knowledge remote set" " " url " [" \-\-bundle " " D "]"
Add or replace the selected bundle's origin URL.
.SS monitor
.TP
.BR "pix monitor" " [" name "] [" \-\-port " " N "] [" \-\-bind " " ADDR "]"
Live-follow a running sandbox's out-of-sandbox traffic (model requests and
responses, tool and MCP calls) in a host TUI.  Binds
.B :11437
by default.  The optional
.I name
filters to one sandbox by name or ID substring; without it, every sandbox is
shown.  Quit with
.BR q .
.TP
.BI \-\-port " N"
Override the hub port (default 11437).
.TP
.BI \-\-bind " ADDR"
Override the hub's listen address (default
.BR 127.0.0.1 ,
loopback-only).  Linux hosts watching a real sandbox may need
.BR "\-\-bind 0.0.0.0" ", since"
.B host.docker.internal
maps to the bridge gateway there rather than to loopback (unlike Docker
Desktop on macOS/Windows).  A non-loopback bind exposes the monitor hub, with
.B no authentication ,
serving the sandbox's full agent context and tool output to your local
network.
.SS agent
Manage subagents as first-class objects.  An agent stores an
.B intent
(a complexity tier), not a pinned model; the router derives its default model
from a hand-maintained scorecard.  Agents live in
.B ./agents
(or
.BR $PIX_AGENTS_DIR ).
.TP
.BR "pix agent ls" " [" \-\-json "]"
List the roster with each agent's resolved model and WHY (its intent, and
whether the resolver fell back).
.TP
.BR "pix agent new" " " NAME " [" \-\-intent " " I "] [" \-\-description " " D "] [" \-\-tools " " a,b "] [" \-\-budget " " USD "] [" \-\-interactive "]"
Scaffold an agent markdown file.
.TP
.BR "pix agent edit" " " NAME " [" \-\-intent " " I "] [" \-\-description " " D "] [" \-\-tools " " a,b "] [" \-\-budget " " USD "] [" \-\-model " " M "]"
Change fields without hand-editing frontmatter.
.TP
.BR "pix agent rm" " " NAME " " \-\-yes
Remove an agent.
.TP
.BR "pix agent reassess" " [" \-\-model " " NEW "]"
Re-resolve the roster under the current policy/scorecard (zero spend) and
recompile.
.BR \-\-model
points you at hand-editing the scorecard instead of measuring anything.  Prints
the routing diff.
.SS route
The model router: turn a declared
.B intent
(a hard cost/latency/accuracy constraint) into a concrete model, from a registry
of callable models and their prices plus a measured scorecard.  Replaces
hand-pinning a model on every agent.  Execes the sibling
.B pix-host
binary.
.TP
.BR "pix route pick" " " intent " [" \-\-json "]"
Resolve one intent to a model and print the rationale + ranked alternatives.
.TP
.BR "pix route compile" " [" \-\-out " " PATH "]"
Resolve every intent and write the intent-to-model map
.RB ( routing.json ).
Use
.B "\-\-out ./routing.json"
to update the baked file, then
.B "make load"
to bake it into the image.
.TP
.BR "pix route show" " [" \-\-json "]"
Print the registry, scorecard, and resolved-intent table.
.TP
.BR "pix route models" " [" \-\-json "]"
List the model registry.
.SS mcp
MCP runs through sbx's local data-plane gateway (always available, no
.BR SBX_MCP_URL ).
.TP
.BR "pix mcp register" " [" name... "]"
Register named local stdio MCP servers with the sbx gateway.  With no names,
use the configured MCP list.  Remote gateway-catalog names are
skipped (register those with
.BR "pix mcp bundle" ).
.TP
.B "pix mcp ls"
Execute
.BR "sbx mcp ls" .
If
.B sbx
is unavailable, print the command to run on the host.
.TP
.BR "pix mcp load" " " name " [" DIR "]"
Attach an already-registered server to the RUNNING sandbox for DIR (default
cwd) live, with no recreate
.RB ( "sbx mcp load" ).
.TP
.BR "pix mcp auth" " [" args... "]"
Authorize remote OAuth servers via the hosted control plane
.RB ( "sbx mcp auth" ";" " e.g. " "auth --all" ).
.TP
.BR "pix mcp bundle" " [" ls|rm... "]"
Register the shipped public catalog bundle (notion/atlassian/granola) in one
step; other args forward to
.BR "sbx mcp bundle" .
.SS gworkspace
Google Workspace via the host-run google-workspace MCP server (a LOCAL stdio server the sbx
gateway spawns on the host; sbx's native
.B "mcp auth"
does not apply to its Google grant).
.TP
.BR "pix gworkspace setup|status|disable" " [" \-\-account " " email "] [" \-\-credentials " " path "] [" \-\-yes "]"
Guided Google Workspace onboarding: checks the installed dependency CLI, imports your
own Desktop OAuth client JSON via that CLI itself, authorizes the account
REQUESTING READ-ONLY OAUTH SCOPES at grant time (fails if the installed dependency
cannot advertise \-\-readonly for the selected auth route), verifies the exact
hardened headless command the gateway will spawn, registers it with the sbx
gateway, and only then saves config.  Idempotent; requires sbx.
.SS slack
Slack via the host-run slack MCP server (a LOCAL stdio server the sbx gateway
spawns on the host).  SLACK_TOKEN is always a single named person's
.B xoxp\-
user token, never a shared team/bot token
.RB ( docs/design/slack\-setup.md ).
.TP
.BR "pix slack setup" " [" \-\-client\-id " " ID "] [" \-\-redirect\-uri " " URI "] [" \-\-vault " " NAME "] [" \-\-yes "]"
Runs the primary localhost PKCE OAuth flow, verifies and pins the Slack
identity, stores the rotating credential in 1Password, and registers the host
MCP server.  No client secret is required.  Twelve\-hour access tokens refresh
automatically on use.
.TP
.BR "pix slack auth" " [" \-\-client\-id " " ID "] [" \-\-redirect\-uri " " URI "] [" \-\-vault " " NAME "] [" \-\-yes "]"
Authorizes or reauthorizes the browser PKCE grant.  This is needed for initial
consent and the roughly monthly grant renewal, not every 12 hours.
.TP
.BR "pix slack setup" " " \-\-token\-ref " " op://vault/item/field " [" \-\-yes "]"
Static fallback for an existing personal
.B xoxp\-
token.  Never accepts or prints a raw token; verifies the live identity before
writing the 1Password reference and identity pins.
.TP
.B "pix slack status"
Reports credential mode, live identity and pin match, OAuth expiry when
applicable, and gateway registration separately from sandbox attachment.
.TP
.B "pix slack disable"
Removes the Pix\-owned registration and local configuration.  In OAuth mode it
first revokes the grant at Slack and archives the 1Password document.  In
static mode it does not revoke the externally issued token.
.SS pack
A pack is a git-backed context bundle (skills + knowledge + reference-only
integrations + config).  See
.B docs/design/packs.md .
.TP
.BR "pix pack new" " [" PATH "]"
Adopt a pre-existing repo (or one already carrying pack.toml) as a pack, else
git-init a fresh one.  Default PATH is the default pack root.
.TP
.BR "pix pack add" " " "skill|knowledge" " " name " [" PATH "]"
Add an artifact to a pack (implicit-creates it).
.TP
.BR "pix pack use" " " path-or-git-url-or-default
Set the active pack; a git URL is cloned (optional #ref pin).
.B default
is a built-in alias for the default pack root (not
.IR $PWD/default );
.B personal
also works as a deprecated alias (prints a deprecation warning).
.TP
.BR "pix pack ls" ;
.BR "pix pack show" " [" PATH "];"
.BR "pix pack rm"
Inspect the active pack, show a pack, or detach the active pack (files
untouched).
.SS secret
With no subcommand,
.B secret
runs
.BR ls .
.TP
.B "pix secret ls"
Report whether
.B op
is installed and has an account, and classify configured refs without printing
secret values.
.TP
.BI "pix secret set " ENV_VAR " " op://vault/item/field
Upsert a ref into op-refs.env, seeding the refs-only template first if absent.
Rejects a value that is not an
.B op://
reference unless
.I ENV_VAR
is on the documented non-secret allowlist.  A raw space in the ref (a spaced
1Password field name, e.g.
.IR "api key" ,
not
.IR api%20key )
is kept as a literal space:
.B op read
and
.B "op run --env-file"
both reject a percent-encoded
.B %20 .
.TP
.BI "pix secret rm " ENV_VAR
Remove a ref from op-refs.env, preserving every other line.  A no-op
(exit 0) if the key or the file is absent.
.TP
.B "pix secret check"
Resolve each active
.B op://
reference using
.B op read
and print only OK or FAIL.  Missing refs state or unavailable
.B op
returns status 3; failed resolutions return status 1.
.SS config
With no subcommand,
.B config
runs
.BR show .
.TP
.B "pix config show"
Print the resolved configuration path followed by decoded TOML with defaults
applied.
.TP
.BR "pix config path" " [" op-refs "]"
Print the path to
.B config.toml
or, with
.BR op-refs ,
the path to
.BR op-refs.env .
.TP
.BR "pix config get" " " key
Print one resolved value with no decoration for scripts to source (list keys
print space-separated).  Unknown keys fail with a nonzero exit.
.TP
.BR "pix config set" " " key " " value
Set a scalar key or add a value to a list key, then save the configuration.
.TP
.BR "pix config unset" " " key " [" value "]"
Clear or reset a scalar key, or remove
.I value
from a list key.
.PP
Base keys are
.BR google_workspace_account ,
.BR mcp ,
.BR services ,
.BR knowledge_bundles ,
.BR memory_watcher_model ,
.BR memory_embed_model ,
.BR ollama_bridge_model ,
.BR host.enabled ,
.BR host.autonomy ,
and
.BR host.autoserve .
Every server in
.B mcp
is passed to sbx as
.B \-\-static\-mcp
when the sandbox is created, so its tools are in context from the start.
There is no per-server eager/lazy split to configure: attach a server to an
already-running sandbox with
.B "pix mcp load"
instead.
.B ollama_bridge_model
is the local model the sandbox exposes to pi and the router's local option;
by default the memory watcher shares it.
.B run_intent
is the default routing intent for the top-level interactive session (the
overlord that orchestrates the subagent crew); when neither
.B \-\-model
nor
.B \-\-intent
is passed,
.B "pix run"
resolves it through the router to pick the session model (the stack ships
run_intent=overlord, which resolves to GPT-5.6 Sol).
.B host.enabled
gates the unsandboxed
.B "pix host"
mode (default false), and
.B host.autonomy
is reserved for the host-guard strictness.
.B host.autoserve
(default true) gates the launcher's lazy auto-start of the services daemon;
the
.B PIX_NO_AUTOSERVE
environment variable also disables it and wins over the config flag.
.B slack.client_id
is the Slack app's PUBLIC OAuth client id (no secret) used by
.BR "pix slack setup" ;
unsetting it also clears
.BR slack.oauth_vault_id ,
.BR slack.oauth_document_id ,
and
.B slack.oauth_grant_expires_at
so a stale locator can never point at a credential minted under a different
client id.
.B slack.redirect_uri
is the registered OAuth callback URL; unsetting it restores the built-in
default when a client id is configured, else clears it.
.BR slack.oauth_vault_id ,
.BR slack.oauth_document_id ,
and
.B slack.oauth_grant_expires_at
are managed state written only by
.BR "pix slack setup" :
.B "pix config get"
reads them for diagnostics, but
.B set
and
.B unset
refuse them directly (use
.B "pix slack setup"
to reauthorize, or
.B "pix config unset slack.client_id"
to clear the whole group).
Keys are
.BR google_workspace_account ,
.BR mcp ,
.BR knowledge_bundles ,
.BR kit
(the alias
.B kits
is accepted),
.BR services ,
.BR memory_watcher_model ,
.BR memory_embed_model ,
.BR ollama_bridge_model ,
.BR host.enabled ,
.BR host.autonomy ,
.BR host.autoserve ,
.BR slack.client_id ,
.BR slack.redirect_uri ,
.BR slack.oauth_vault_id ,
.BR slack.oauth_document_id ,
and
.BR slack.oauth_grant_expires_at .
Setting
.B knowledge_bundles
enables the knowledge service.  Switching the active pack
.RB ( "pix pack use" )
swaps the whole config facet set at once, not just skills; see
.BR "pix pack" .
.SS task
Run parallel tasks on one repository.  Each task is a
.B git clone --local
of the repo with its own branch and sandbox, so tasks never collide.  Commits
land in the task's clone on this host and can be pushed or fetched back.  Clones
live under
.B $XDG_STATE_HOME/pix/tasks/<repo>/co/<name>
outside your repo.
.TP
.BR "pix task new" " " name " [" \-\-from " " REF "] [" \-\- " pi-args..." ]
Clone the repo, create the branch
.BR pix/ name ,
and launch a sandbox for it.
.B \-\-from
accepts any branch, tag, or commit that exists in the repo (default
.BR HEAD ).
Everything after
.B \-\-
is passed to pi.
.TP
.BR "pix task ls" " [" \-\-json "]"
List this repo's tasks with their branch, sandbox status, and git state
(dirty and unpushed markers).  Read-only.
.TP
.BR "pix task path" " " name
Print the task's checkout directory and nothing else, so it composes in a
shell:
.BR "cd \"$(pix task " name " path)\"" .
The convenience form
.B "pix task" name "path"
is also accepted.  A missing task exits non-zero with an empty stdout.
.TP
.BR "pix task rm" " " name " [" \-\-force "]"
Tear down the sandbox and the clone, guarded.  It refuses uncommitted or
unpushed work and prints the exact recovery command unless
.B \-\-force
is given.  It always snapshots the task branch to
.B refs/pix/recovered/<name>
in the main repo first, and aborts before deleting the clone if
.B sbx rm
fails.  The main repo's branches are never touched.  After the sandbox is gone
and before the clone is deleted, it harvests any uncommitted doc files (see
.B "task harvest" ).
.TP
.BR "pix task gc" " [" \-\-days " " N "] [" \-\-dry\-run "] [" \-\-no\-harvest "] [" \-\-artifact\-days " " N "]"
Prune clean tasks whose age exceeds
.B \-\-days
(default 7; age is the newer of the task's creation time and its checkout's git
activity).  It reuses the same guard as
.BR rm ,
so a dirty, unpushed, or still-running task is skipped and reported, never
dropped (there is no
.BR \-\-force ).
.B \-\-dry\-run
reports what would happen.  It also prunes harvested artifact snapshots older
than
.B \-\-artifact\-days
(default 30).
.TP
.BR "pix task harvest" " " name " [" \-\-to " " DIR "]"
Copy the task's uncommitted doc files (untracked, ignored, or modified-vs-HEAD)
to
.B $XDG_DATA_HOME/pix/artifacts/<repo>/<name>/<timestamp>/
with a
.B manifest.json
of provenance.  Idempotent and safe to run any time.  With
.B \-\-to
it copies to a caller-chosen dir instead (refused if that dir is the checkout
itself).  Harvested docs live under
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
so they survive a
.BR "pix state reset" .
.SS state
The
.B state
noun groups the stack's on-disk lifecycle commands.  The flat spellings
.BR backup ,
.BR restore ,
and
.BR reset
keep working as aliases.
.TP
.B "pix state backup"
Alias for
.BR backup .
.TP
.B "pix state restore"
Alias for
.BR restore .
.TP
.B "pix state reset"
Alias for
.BR reset .
.SS reset
.TP
.BR "pix reset" " [" \-\-keep-memory "] [" \-\-purge-data "] [" \-\-sbx "] [" \-\-force "] [" \-\-yes "|" \-y "]"
Stop host services and move configuration and data aside to timestamped
.B .bak-*
siblings.  Nothing is hard-deleted.
.B \-\-keep-memory
preserves captured memory while resetting other state.
.B \-\-purge-data
also moves aside harvested task artifacts, which are kept by default.
.B \-\-sbx
also attempts, on a best-effort basis, to remove all
.B pix-*
sandboxes and unregister configured MCP servers.  Failures in those sbx actions
are reported but do not fail the reset.
.B \-\-force
allows data movement if the service still appears to be running.
.BR \-\-yes
or
.B \-y
suppresses confirmation and is required on a non-interactive terminal.
.SS "version"
.TP
.B "pix version"
Print the stamped launcher version.  The aliases
.B \-v
and
.B \-\-version
are accepted.
.SS help
.TP
.BR "pix help" " [" verb "]"
Print general help or the authoritative usage text for an implemented verb.
.B \-h
and
.B \-\-help
are top-level aliases.
.SS man
.TP
.B "pix man"
Render this manual page.  The same page is also produced by
.B pix help --man
(the folded invocation) and by the global
.B \-\-man
alias.  The page is embedded in the binary, so no
.B MANPATH
entry is required.  The rendered page is produced through the first available
of
.BR mandoc (1),
.BR man (1),
or
.BR groff (1);
if none is present the raw roff source is written out.  When standard output is
not a terminal, the page is written directly without a pager, so
.B "pix man"
can be piped or redirected.  The global alias
.B \-\-man
is accepted anywhere on the command line.
.SH OPTIONS
.SH FILES
.SS "Configuration tree"
.TP
.B ~/.config/pix/config.toml
Machine-managed TOML configuration.  The directory and file may move under
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
or
.BR PIX_CONFIG .
Schema fields are
.BR version_pin ,
.BR services ,
.BR mcp ,
.BR memory_watcher_model ,
.BR memory_embed_model ,
.BR google_workspace_account ,
.BR knowledge_bundles ,
.BR kits.stack ,
.BR skills.paths ,
.BR pack ,
and
.BR plugins.* .
.TP
.B ~/.config/pix/op-refs.env
Refs-only environment file for host MCP credentials.  Secret entries are
.B op://vault/item/field
references resolved by
.BR op (1).
.TP
.B ~/.config/pix/broker-token
Mode-0600 bearer shared by launcher and host broker components.
.TP
.B ~/.config/pix/serve.pid
PID of the running
.B pix-host serve
supervisor.
.TP
.B ~/.config/pix/knowledge/
Default global OKF bundle created by
.BR "knowledge init" .
.TP
.B ~/.config/pix/knowledge-cache/
Cached clones for knowledge bundle Git URLs.
.SS "Data tree"
.TP
.B ~/.local/share/pix/memory/memory.db
Default memory SQLite database.
.TP
.B ~/.local/share/pix/knowledge/knowledge.db
Default knowledge SQLite index.
.TP
.B ~/.local/share/pix/backups/pix-backup-*.tar.gz
Default full backup archives (memory, config, and op-refs).
.SS "Workspace and installation files"
.TP
.B .pix/knowledge
Committed per-project knowledge bundle pointer.
.TP
.B .pix/knowledge.scope
Launcher-generated canonical bundle scope for the current run.
.PP
.B pix run
and
.B pix task new
add generated
.B .pix
workspace state to the repository's local
.B .git/info/exclude
file. The
.B .pix/knowledge
pointer remains trackable.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PIX_CONFIG
Use this exact path for
.BR config.toml .
Its parent also contains
.BR op-refs.env ,
.BR broker-token ,
and
.BR serve.pid .
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Use
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/pix
as the configuration directory when
.B PIX_CONFIG
is unset.
.TP
.B PIX_DEV_ROOT
Directory of a pix checkout used by
.BR "run --dev" .
It must contain
.BR pi-kit/spec.yaml .
.TP
.B PIX_DEBUG
When non-empty, print the composed
.B sbx
command before running it.
.TP
.B PIX_OP_REFS
Override the refs file included by
.BR backup .
This does not change the canonical refs path used by
.BR "secret" .
.TP
.B MEMORY_DB
Override the memory database path, normally
.BR ~/.local/share/pix/memory/memory.db .
Reset, backup, and restore honor it.
.TP
.B KNOWLEDGE_DB
Override the knowledge index path, normally
.BR ~/.local/share/pix/knowledge/knowledge.db .
Reset honors it.
.TP
.B MEMORY_PORT
Override memory port 11435 for the host service, launcher RPC client, status,
reset guard, restore service probe, and serve control.
.TP
.B KNOWLEDGE_PORT
Override knowledge port 11436 for the host service, launcher RPC client, and
serve control.
.TP
.B MEMORY_WATCHER_MODEL
Override the Ollama fact-capture model.  The configuration default is
.BR gemma3:4b
(small, so the resident watcher fits a 16GB machine).
.TP
.B MEMORY_EMBED_MODEL
Override the Ollama embedding model used by memory and knowledge.  The
configuration default is
.BR nomic-embed-text .
.TP
.B KNOWLEDGE_BUNDLES
Comma- or platform path-list-separated bundle paths used by the host supervisor
only when configured knowledge bundles are empty.
.TP
.B SBX_MCP_URL
Optional cloud/SaaS MCP gateway URL.  No longer required: sbx's local
data-plane gateway serves MCP (registration + attachment) without it.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, displayed status, an accepted abort, or help.
.TP
.B 1
Configuration, filesystem, Git, subprocess, RPC-response, or other operational
failure.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command, option, argument, or other usage error.
.TP
.B 3
A required or explicitly requested readiness check cannot be verified from the
current environment; no failure was positively observed.  Memory and knowledge
RPC commands also use this status when their daemon cannot be reached.
.PP
Commands that execute
.BR sbx ,
.BR pix-host ,
or another child process may propagate that process's exit status.
.SH EXAMPLES
Apply host-side config non-interactively (CI); conversational onboarding is
in-session via
.BR run :
.PP
.nf
.RS
pix setup --no-agent --knowledge ~/knowledge --yes
.RE
.fi
.PP
Launch the current directory, then launch a project under a different pack:
.PP
.nf
.RS
pix run
pix pack use work
pix run ~/src/project
.RE
.fi
.PP
Recall and record memory:
.PP
.nf
.RS
pix memory recall "release process" --limit 5
pix memory remember "Release tags use the v prefix"
.RE
.fi
.PP
Push knowledge changes to a review branch:
.PP
.nf
.RS
pix knowledge sync -m "knowledge: update release process"
.RE
.fi
.PP
Set a 1Password reference and stop host services:
.PP
.nf
.RS
pix secret set SLACK_TOKEN op://Private/Slack/credential
pix serve stop
.RE
.fi
.PP
Reset state while preserving captured memory:
.PP
.nf
.RS
pix reset --keep-memory
.RE
.fi
.PP
Back up and restore the full stack (memory, config, op-refs):
.PP
.nf
.RS
pix backup --out ~/backups/pix.tar.gz
pix serve stop
pix restore ~/backups/pix.tar.gz --force
.RE
.fi
.PP
Run two isolated tasks on one repo in parallel, then tear one down:
.PP
.nf
.RS
pix task new fix-login
pix task new refactor-db --from origin/main
pix task ls
pix task rm fix-login
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pi (1),
.BR sbx (1),
.BR op (1)
